>
<h1>Vacuum Conveyors</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">A vacuum conveyor uses negative air pressure to hold flat products against a perforated belt surface or to draw bulk materials through enclosed tubing. Vacuum belt conveyors grip sheets, labels, film, and pouches that standard conveyors cannot handle reliably. Vacuum tube conveyors transport powders, granules, and pellets through sealed pipelines to <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/">packaging equipment</a>, mixers, and process hoppers. Both types use suction, as either a holding force or a motive force, to solve handling problems no other conveyor technology can.</p>
<h2 id="belt-heading">Vacuum Belt Conveyors</h2>
<h3>How Vacuum Belt Conveyors Work</h3>
<p>A vacuum belt conveyor consists of a perforated belt running over a vacuum plenum (suction box). A blower or vacuum pump removes air from the plenum, creating negative pressure beneath the belt. Atmospheric pressure above the belt pushes products down against the belt surface through the perforations. This suction force holds products firmly against the belt during horizontal transport, incline/decline travel, and even inverted (upside-down) conveying.</p>
<p>The belt perforation pattern and plenum zone layout control where suction is active. Zones can be turned on and off to grip or release products at specific locations. Variable vacuum levels adjust holding force for different product weights and porosities. Heavier or more porous products require stronger vacuum; lighter or non-porous products need less.</p>
<h3>Vacuum Belt Applications</h3>
<p><strong>Sheet feeding and transport:</strong> Paper sheets, cardboard blanks, corrugated flats, and plastic sheets ride vacuum belt conveyors from cutting stations to printing, folding, and converting machines. The vacuum holds sheets flat, preventing curling, wrinkling, and flutter at high speeds.</p>
<p><strong>Label and film handling:</strong> Die-cut labels, shrink sleeves, and packaging film segments travel on vacuum belts between cutting, printing, and application stations. The vacuum grips these thin, lightweight materials that would blow off a standard belt.</p>
<p><strong>Pouch and sachet transport:</strong> Flexible pouches and sachets ride vacuum belts from filling machines to cartoning or case packing. The vacuum prevents pouches from shifting, overlapping, or flying off the belt during transport and accumulation. See our <a href="/conveyor-functions/bag-loading/">bag loading conveyor guide</a> for complete coverage of bag handling and case packing infeed systems.</p>
<p><strong>Incline and decline transport:</strong> Vacuum holding lets flat products travel up or down inclines without sliding or tumbling. This replaces <a href="/conveyor-types/cleated-conveyors/">cleated belts</a> for flat products where cleats would damage the product surface.</p>
<p><strong>Inverted conveying:</strong> Vacuum belts carry products upside-down beneath the belt for underside printing, labeling, inspection, or transferring products over obstacles. The vacuum force overcomes gravity to hold products against the inverted belt surface.</p>
<h2 id="tube-heading">Vacuum Tube Conveyors</h2>
<h3>How Vacuum Tube Conveyors Work</h3>
<p>A vacuum tube conveyor is a <a href="/conveyor-types/pneumatic-conveyors/">pneumatic conveying</a> system that uses vacuum (negative pressure) as the motive force. A vacuum pump at the destination creates suction that draws material from the pickup point through sealed tubing. Material enters the system through a suction nozzle, wand, or hopper adapter with no mechanical feed device required at the source. At the destination, a filter receiver separates material from air. <a href="https://www.volkmannusa.com/pneumatic-vacuum-conveyors/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Volkmann describes the sequence</a> as suction air drawing material through the piping from storage, a filter module separating dust and product from the conveying air, and a pneumatically operated flap valve discharging product into the destination equipment.</p>
<h3>Batch vs. Continuous Operation</h3>
<p>Most vacuum tube conveyors operate in batch (cycle) mode. The vacuum pump runs for a set time, drawing material into the receiver. Then the vacuum stops, and a discharge valve opens, dropping the collected material into the process hopper below. The cycle repeats. This batch operation provides accurate material metering and prevents material from entering the vacuum pump. Continuous vacuum conveying (used in larger pneumatic systems) uses rotary airlock valves for continuous discharge.</p>
<h3>Vacuum Tube Applications</h3>
<p><strong>Feeding packaging machines:</strong> Vacuum tube conveyors feed powders and granules from bulk storage to filling machines, multi-head weighers, and bagging equipment. The compact receiver mounts directly on the machine hopper, minimizing floor space. Throughput depends on material, distance, tube size, and pump, so get a rated figure from the supplier for your material and layout.</p>
<p><strong>Drum, bag, and bulk bag unloading:</strong> Suction wands draw material from open drums, sacks, and bulk bags (FIBCs) without dust exposure. The operator inserts the wand into the container, and vacuum transfers the material to the destination. This eliminates manual scooping, pouring, and dust-cloud exposure.</p>
<p><strong>Tablet and capsule transfer:</strong> Pharmaceutical vacuum conveyors gently move finished tablets and capsules between process steps (coating, inspection, bottle filling) with minimal breakage. The low conveying velocity and short distance of vacuum tube systems minimize tablet chipping and dust generation.</p>
<p><strong>Multi-floor ingredient transfer:</strong> Vacuum tube systems lift ingredients vertically through floors using small-diameter tubes that penetrate walls and floors with minimal construction impact. One vacuum receiver per floor serves multiple machines. This replaces elevators, vertical screws, and <a href="/conveyor-types/bucket-elevators/">bucket elevators</a> in multi-story <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">food</a> and pharmaceutical plants.</p>
<h2 id="spec-heading">Specifying Vacuum Conveyors</h2>
<h3>Vacuum Belt Sizing</h3>
<p>Vacuum belt conveyor sizing depends on product dimensions, weight, porosity, speed, and whether the conveyor runs horizontal, inclined, or inverted. Key specifications include: belt width (wider than the product so the edges are held), belt perforation size and pattern (must not mark the product), vacuum level (set by product weight, porosity, and orientation), and blower capacity (airflow must maintain vacuum with the expected leakage through open perforations).</p>
<h3>Vacuum Tube Sizing</h3>
<p>Vacuum tube conveyor sizing depends on material properties (bulk density, particle size, flowability), required throughput (lbs/hr), conveying distance (horizontal and vertical), and number of bends. Larger tube diameters increase throughput but require more powerful vacuum pumps. Manufacturers provide sizing software that calculates tube diameter, pump size, and cycle time for specific material and layout combinations.</p>
<h3>Vacuum Generation</h3>
<p>Vacuum belt conveyors use regenerative blowers, side-channel blowers, or dedicated vacuum fans. Vacuum tube conveyors use multi-stage ejector pumps (powered by compressed air), regenerative blowers, or rotary claw pumps. Compressed-air ejectors are simple and maintenance-free but consume plant air. Electric vacuum pumps generally use less energy for continuous operation.</p>
<h3>Material Compatibility</h3>
<p>Vacuum belt conveyors suit non-porous or low-porosity flat products. Highly porous products (like corrugated board) require more vacuum to compensate for air leakage through the product. Vacuum tube conveyors suit free-flowing dry bulk solids. Sticky, hygroscopic, or high-moisture materials may bridge in receivers or coat tube walls. Material testing with the conveyor manufacturer is recommended for unfamiliar materials. If the material is a combustible dust, the receiver, filter, and pump need to be designed for that hazard. <a href="https://www.osha.gov/combustible-dust" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">OSHA's combustible dust page</a> explains how finely divided combustible material can become explosible when suspended in air.</p>
<h2 id="pros-heading">Advantages &amp; Limitations</h2>
<p><strong>Vacuum belt advantages:</strong> Positive grip on flat/thin products. Handles incline, decline, and inverted transport. Prevents flutter and shifting at high speeds. Precise product placement for downstream registration. Adjustable vacuum zones for different product sizes on the same conveyor.</p>
<p><strong>Vacuum belt limitations:</strong> Higher cost than standard belt conveyors. Blower noise may need silencers or enclosures, since an 8-hour average of 85 dBA triggers a hearing conservation program under <a href="https://www.osha.gov/laws-regs/regulations/standardnumber/1910/1910.95" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">OSHA 29 CFR 1910.95</a>. Perforated belt replacement is more expensive than standard belt. Not suited for heavy, three-dimensional products where side support is needed. Belt perforations can mark soft products if vacuum is too high.</p>
<p><strong>Vacuum tube advantages:</strong> Dust-free enclosed transport. Compact footprint: tubes fit through tight spaces. No mechanical feed device needed at source. Simple, clean design with few moving parts. Inherently safe (leaks draw air in, not blow material out).</p>
<p><strong>Vacuum tube limitations:</strong> Conveying distance is limited because the available pressure differential is capped by the vacuum level. Lower throughput than pressure pneumatic systems. Batch cycling reduces average continuous capacity. Higher energy cost per ton than mechanical conveyors. Material must be free-flowing and dry. Abrasive materials wear tubes and elbows at pickup velocity.</p>
