<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What is a vacuum conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Two distinct types: vacuum belt conveyors use suction through a perforated belt to hold flat/thin products (sheets, labels, film); vacuum tube conveyors draw bulk materials (powders, granules) through sealed tubes using a vacuum pump. Both use negative air pressure as the holding or transport force.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">Vacuum belt vs. vacuum tube conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Vacuum belt: perforated belt holds flat items against the surface for transport (sheets, labels, pouches). Vacuum tube: sealed pipeline draws bulk powders and granules from source to destination. Completely different applications despite sharing the "vacuum" name.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">What products suit vacuum belt conveyors?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Flat, thin, flexible, and lightweight products: paper sheets, cardboard blanks, plastic film, labels, foil, fabric, pouches, sachets, and thin gaskets. Products that must remain flat during transport or would blow off a standard belt at speed.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">How does a vacuum tube conveyor work?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A vacuum pump at the destination creates suction that draws material from the pickup point through sealed tubing. Material separates from air in a filter receiver and drops into the process hopper. Most operate in batch cycles: vacuum-on draws material, vacuum-off discharges. See our <a href="/conveyor-types/pneumatic-conveyors/">pneumatic conveyor guide</a> for full system details.</p>

</div>
</div>
